  "worked_solution": "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \u27e81-{e}^{t/2},3\u27e9\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e8\\int (1-{e}^{t/2})\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 3\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8t-2{e}^{t/2}+{C}_{1},3t+{C}_{2}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(2)=\u27e80,1\u27e9.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =\u27e82-2{e}^{2/2}+{C}_{1},3(2)+{C}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e80,1\u27e9 & =\u27e82-2e+{C}_{1},6+{C}_{2}\u27e9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=2e-2$ and ${C}_{2}=-5.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\u27e8t-2{e}^{t/2}+2e-2,3t-5\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =\u27e80-2{e}^{0/2}+2e-2,3(0)-5\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82e-4,-5\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
